      "notes": "Global WAL-mode SQLite store (+ -wal/-shm) written by the native runtime; mixed provenance. sessions, turns, checkpoints, session_files, session_refs, search_index* and dynamic_context_items are derived from events.jsonl (rebuilt by `/chronicle reindex`) and repeat transcript content: MUST-NOT-PARSE. assistant_usage_events (since CLI 1.0.69, changelog 'Show exact local assistant usage in Chronicle and session SQL') is NOT derived: one row per model request, written live by the runtime's usage processor from the ephemeral assistant.usage event that events.jsonl never persists, never updated or deleted (the runtime's DELETE statements touch only search_index, dynamic_context_items and inbox_entries). Columns: id, session_id, turn_index, agent_id, parent_tool_call_id, model, input_tokens (includes cache reads and writes), output_tokens (includes reasoning), cache_read_tokens, cache_write_tokens (1.0.88: 5-minute plus 1-hour writes), reasoning_tokens, total_nano_aiu (10^9 nano-AIU per AI credit, one credit = $0.01; 0 or NULL when GitHub did not meter the call, e.g. BYOK), request_multiplier, duration/latency columns, initiator, api_endpoint, reasoning_effort, finish_reason, content_filter_triggered, token_details_json (the CAPI copilot_usage tokenDetails array: tokenType in input | cache_read | cache_write | cache_write_1h | output with exclusive tokenCount, batchSize, costPerBatch; 1.0.88 entries may carry a model), created_at (ISO 8601 UTC text). The 1.0.88 runtime migrates in copilot_usage_model (the billing model) and output_ttft_ms. The adapter's snapshot plan reads ONLY this table. Evidence: structure-only observed census of a 1.0.80-written store (schema_version 6) plus the runtime DDL for 1.0.80 and 1.0.88; the farm still strips this database at harvest, so a hermetic capture that keeps it is pending. Structure-only census of a live organic dataset. Shapes sampled from the first 256 records per type; nested facet shapes from the first 32 records per nested discriminator; observed counts cover the full tree.",
